342	What property of~$A$ and~$B$ allows us to conclude that
343	\begin{equation}\label{eq:can-we-use-approx-delta-to-derive-this}
344	B^x_a \ot I \simeq_{\eps} I \ot C^x_a?
345	\end{equation}
346	The answer is provided by the \emph{state-dependent distance}.
347	
348	\begin{definition}[The state-dependent distance]\label{def:approx_delta}
349	Let $\ket{\psi}$ be a state in $\calH$.
350	Let $A = \{A^x_a\}$ and $B = \{B^x_a\}$ be
351	sets of matrices acting on $\calH$.
352	Finally, let $\calD$ be a distribution on the question set~$\calX$.
353	Then we say that
354	  \begin{equation*}
355	  A^x_a \approx_{\delta}  B^x_a
356	  \end{equation*}
357	 on state $\ket{\psi}$ and distribution $\calD$ if
358	  \begin{equation*}
359	  \E_{\bx \sim \calD} \sum_{a} \Vert (A^{\bx}_a - B^{\bx}_a) \ket{\psi} \Vert^2 \leq \delta.
360	    \end{equation*}
361	\end{definition}
362	
363	We note one odd feature of \Cref{def:approx_delta} in comparison to the consistency,
364	which is that $\ket{\psi}$ is not assumed to have a bipartition $\calH_{\mathrm{A}} \ot \calH_{\mathrm{B}}$
365	in which~$A$ and~$B$ are applied on opposite sides.
366	We will address this in \Cref{sec:consistency-from-state-dependent-distance} below.
